                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Blakers-Massey+theorem"} Blakers-Massey theorem
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+homotopy+van+Kampen+theorem"} higher homotopy van Kampen theorem
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve+theorem"} nerve theorem
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead%27s+theorem"} Whitehead's theorem
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurewicz+theorem"} Hurewicz theorem
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+theory"} Galois theory
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis"}> homotopy hypothesis
                        \-theorem
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#presentations_of_infinitygroupoids"} Presentations of infinity-groupoids
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ordered_cubical_sets"} Ordered cubical sets
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#other_cubical_sets"} Other cubical sets
              %li
                %a{:href => "#joyaltype_model_structures"} Joyal-type model structures
              %li
                %a{:href => "#cubicaltype_model_structures"} Cubical-type model structures
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#cubical_sets_without_connections"} Cubical sets without connections
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#cubical_sets_with_maxconnections"} Cubical sets with max-connections
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#cubical_sets_with_maxconnections_and_minconnections"} Cubical sets with max-connections and min-connections
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#cartesian_cubical_sets"} Cartesian cubical sets
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Various
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            can be presented by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structures"} model structures
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mo □
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Box^{op},Set]
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+sets"} cubical sets
            = succeed "," do
              %em i.e.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+cubes"} category of cubes
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
          %p
            For example, the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/test+categories"} test categories
            produces model structures on categories of cubical sets whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            is that of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} classical model structure on simplicial sets
            = succeed "-theorem," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis"} homotopy hypothesis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+shapes+for+higher+structures"} geometric shapes for higher structures
            the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cube"} cube
          %p
            Cubical sets can also be used to build model structures presenting other homotopy theories, such the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structures+for+cubical+quasicategories"} model structures for cubical quasicategories
            presenting
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
          %h2#presentations_of_infinitygroupoids Presentations of infinity-groupoids
          %h3#ordered_cubical_sets Ordered cubical sets
          %p
            Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
            for the
            %em ordered cube category
            defined at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+cubes"} category of cubes
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtable{:columnalign => "right center left", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                        %mo □
                      %mtd
                        %mo →
                      %mtd
                        %mi sSet
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                        %msup
                          %mi I
                          %mi n
                      %mtd
                        %mo ↦
                      %mtd
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mrow
                            %mo ×
                            %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{array}{rcl}
                  \\Box &amp;\to&amp; sSet \\
                  I^n &amp;\mapsto&amp; (\Delta[1])^{\times n}
                  \\end{array}
          %p
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cube+category"} cube category
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
            which sends the cubical
            = succeed "-cube" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-cube." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            Similarly, there is a canonical
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtable{:columnalign => "right center left", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                        %mo □
                      %mtd
                        %mo →
                      %mtd
                        %mi Top
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                        %msup
                          %mi I
                          %mi n
                      %mtd
                        %mo ↦
                      %mtd
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{array}{rcl}
                  \\Box &amp;\to&amp; Top \\
                  I^n &amp;\mapsto&amp; [0,1]^{n}
                  \\end{array}
          %p
            The corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve+and+realization"} nerve and realization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msub
                    %mi Sing
                    %mo □
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi Top
                  %mover
                    %munder
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Sing
                          %mo □
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mo −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mo □
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (|-| \dashv Sing_\Box) : Top \stackrel{\overset{|-|}{\leftarrow}}{\underset{Sing_\Box}{\to}} [\Box^{op},Set]
          %p is the cubical analogue of the simplicial nerve and realization adjunction.
          #OrderedCubeModelStructure.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              There is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+cubical+sets"} model structure on cubical sets
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mo □
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Box^{op},Set]
              whose
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  weak equivalences are the morphisms that become weak equivalences under geometric realization
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mo −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |-|
              %li
                %p
                  cofibrations are the
                  = succeed "s." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
          %p
            This is (
            %a{:href => "#Jardine"}> Jardine, section 3
            ).
          .num_theorem
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              An explicit set of generating cofibrations is given by the boundary inclusions
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mo □
                        %mi n
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mo □
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial \Box^n \to \Box^n
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mo ⊓
                        %mrow
                          %mi k
                          %mo ,
                          %mi ϵ
                        %mi n
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mo □
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sqcap_{k,\epsilon}^n \to \Box^n
          %p
            This is (
            %a{:href => "#Cisinski"}> Cisinski 2006, Thm 8.4.38
            ).
          %p
            As a consequence, the fibrations are exactly cubical Kan fibrations. The following theorem establishes a form of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis"} homotopy hypothesis
            for cubical sets.
          #Ordered.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+of+an+adjunction"} unit of the adjunction
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Sing
                      %mo □
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    A \to Sing_\Box(|A|)
            %p
              is a weak equivalence in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mo □
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Box^{op},Set]
              for every cubical set
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %p The counit of the adjunction
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msub
                      %mi Sing
                      %mo □
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    |Sing_\Box X| \to X
            %p
              is a weak equivalence in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
              for every topological space
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %p
              It follows that we have an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
              induced on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+categories"} homotopy categories
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ho
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Top
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi Ho
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mo □
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Ho(Top) \simeq Ho([\Box^{op},Set])
                    \\,.
          %p
            This is (
            %a{:href => "#Jardine"}> Jardine, theorem 29, corollary 30
            ).
          %p
            In fact, by the discussion at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} adjoint (∞,1)-functor
            it follows that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functors"} derived functors
            of the adjunction exhibit the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+localizations"} simplicial localizations
            of cubical sets equivalent to that of simplicial sets, hence makes their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} equivalent
            (hence equivalent to
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
          %h3#other_cubical_sets Other cubical sets
          %p
            The model structure from Theorem
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#OrderedCubeModelStructure"}
            and the equivalence of its homotopy theory to that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            can be derived using the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/test+categories"} test categories
            (see (
            %a{:href => "#Cisinski"}> Cisinski 2006, Section 8.4
            )). Test category theory can be used more generally to derive model structures presenting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Cube
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Cube^{op},Set]
            for other cube categories
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
          %p
            The weak equivalences are not in general induced from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalences"} weak homotopy equivalences
            by the geometric realization functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mo □
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |-| \colon [\Box^{op},Set] \to Top
            sending
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^n
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]^{n}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomason+model+structure"} Thomason model structure
            by the realization
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mo □
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Box^{op},Set] \to Cat
            sending
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^n
            to the slice category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box/I^n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+cofibrations"} trivial cofibrations
            are generated by cubical horn inclusions
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ⊓
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo ,
                        %mi ϵ
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mo □
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sqcap_{k,\epsilon}^n \to \Box^n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reedy+category"} Reedy category
            structure on the cube category.
          %p
            A case of particular interest is the ordered cube category with a connection. Unlike the minimal cube category, this cube category is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+test+category"} strict test category
            (
            %a{:href => "#Maltsiniotis2009"}> Maltsiniotis 2009
            ), meaning its weak equivalences are closed under product. This is one motivation for using cubes with connections as opposed to the minimal cube category; see
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+cubical+set"} connection on a cubical set
            for more details.
          %p
            %a{:href => "#BuchholtzMorehouse2017"} Buchholtz and Morehouse (2017)
            outline a class of cube categories and investigate which are test and/or strict test categories.
          %h2#joyaltype_model_structures Joyal-type model structures
          %p
            In complete analogy to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joyal+model+structure"} Joyal model structure
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+sets"} cubical sets
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structures+for+cubical+quasicategories"} model structures for cubical quasicategories
          %h2#cubicaltype_model_structures Cubical-type model structures
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+type+theory"} cubical type theory
            one uses more structured notions of cubical set (symmetric, cartesian, De Morgan, etc.). In most cases such categories have both a
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                test model structure
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/test+model+structure"} ?
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical-type+model+structure"} cubical-type model structure
            that corresponds to the interpretation of type theory. In many cases these are
            %em not
            equivalent, and the cubical-type model structure does not model classical homotopy types. See
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical-type+model+structure"} cubical-type model structure
            for more discussion.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+set"} cubical set
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+set+with+connections"} cubical set with connections
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/test+category"}> test category
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+test+category"} strict test category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cisinski+model+structure"} Cisinski model structure
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structures+for+cubical+quasicategories"} model structures for cubical quasicategories
          %h2#references References
          %h3#cubical_sets_without_connections Cubical sets without connections
          %p
            Using that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cube+category"} cube category
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/test+category"} test category
            a model structure on cubical sets follows as a special case of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+presheaves+over+a+test+category"} model structure on presheaves over a test category
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Denis-Charles+Cisinski"}> Denis-Charles Cisinski
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/joyalscatlab/published/Les+pr%C3%A9faisceaux+comme+type+d%27homotopie"} Les préfaisceaux comme type d'homotopie
              = succeed "." do
                %strong 308
          %p
            Cisinski also derives explicit generating cofibrations and generating acyclic cofibrations using his theory of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+Reedy+category"} generalized Reedy category
            = succeed "." do
              %em categories skelettiques
          %p The model structure on cubical sets as above is given in detail in
          %ul#Jardine
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.+F.+Jardine"}> J. F. Jardine
              ,
              %em Cubical homotopy theory: a beginning
              (2002) (
              %a{:href => "https://web.archive.org/web/20210507014113/http://hopf.math.purdue.edu/Jardine/cubical2.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %h3#cubical_sets_with_maxconnections Cubical sets with max-connections
          %p
            The following paper proves that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+sets+with+connections"} cubical sets with connections
            (more specifically, max-connections) form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+test+category"} strict test category
            and therefore admit a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+model+structure"} cartesian model structure
            that is Quillen equivalent to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan%E2%80%93Quillen+model+structure"} Kan–Quillen model structure
            on
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
          %ul
            %li#Maltsiniotis2009
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georges+Maltsiniotis"}> Georges Maltsiniotis
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em La catégorie cubique avec connexions est une catégorie test stricte
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.4310/hha.2009.v11.n2.a15"} doi
          %h3#cubical_sets_with_maxconnections_and_minconnections Cubical sets with max-connections and min-connections
          %p
            The case of cubical sets with both max-connections and min-connections largely follows the case of cubical sets with max-connections, the corresponding category of cubes again being a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+test+category"} strict test category
          %ul
            %li#BuchholtzMorehouse2017
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ulrik+Buchholtz"}> Ulrik Buchholtz
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Morehouse"} Edward Morehouse
              = succeed "," do
                %em Varieties of Cubical Sets
              %strong 10226
              (2017) 77–92 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.08189"}> arXiv:1701.08189
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://dx.doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-57418-9_5"} doi:10.1007/978-3-319-57418-9_5
          %h3#cartesian_cubical_sets Cartesian cubical sets
          %p The case of the category of presheaves over the free finite product category with a bipointed object is treated in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Awodey"}> Steve Awodey
              ,
              %em Cartesian cubical model categories
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.00893"}> arXiv:2305.00893
              )
        .revisedby
          %p
